.SH "NAME"
pcre2convert \- PCRE2 pattern conversion
.SH "EXPERIMENTAL PATTERN CONVERSION FUNCTIONS"
.sp
This document describes a set of functions that can be used to convert
"foreign" patterns into PCRE2 regular expressions. This facility is currently
experimental, and may be changed in future releases. Two kinds of pattern,
globs and POSIX patterns, are supported.
.SH "THE CONVERT CONTEXT"
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CRpcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CBpcre2_convert_context_create\fP\f(CR(
      pcre2_general_context *\fP\f(CIgcontext\fP\f(CR);

pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CBpcre2_convert_context_copy\fP\f(CR(
      pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CIccontext\fP\f(CR);

void\fP \f(CBpcre2_convert_context_free\fP\f(CR(pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CIccontext\fP\f(CR);

int\fP \f(CBpcre2_set_glob_escape\fP\f(CR(pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CIccontext\fP\f(CR,
                          uint32_t\fP \f(CIescape_char\fP\f(CR);

int\fP \f(CBpcre2_set_glob_separator\fP\f(CR(pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CIccontext\fP\f(CR,
                             uint32_t\fP \f(CIseparator_char\fP\f(CR);\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
A convert context is used to hold parameters that affect the way that pattern
conversion works. Like all PCRE2 contexts, you need to use a context only if
you want to override the defaults. There are the usual create, copy, and free
functions. If custom memory management functions are set in a general context
that is passed to \f(CBpcre2_convert_context_create()\fP, they are used for all
memory management within the conversion functions.
.sp
There are only two parameters in the convert context at present. Both apply
only to glob conversions. The escape character defaults to grave accent under
Windows, otherwise backslash. It can be set to zero, meaning no escape
character, or to an ASCII punctuation character (or its EBCDIC equivalent),
except \f(CR!\fP, \f(CR*\fP, \f(CR\-\fP, \f(CR:\fP, \f(CR?\fP, \f(CR[\fP, \f(CR]\fP, and \f(CR^\fP. These characters are reserved
for glob wildcard or character\-class syntax and cannot be used as escapes.
The separator character defaults to backslash under Windows, otherwise forward
slash. It can be set to forward slash, backslash, or dot.
.sp
The two setting functions return zero on success, or \f(CBPCRE2_ERROR_BADDATA\fP if
their second argument is invalid.
.SH "THE CONVERSION FUNCTION"
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CRint\fP \f(CBpcre2_pattern_convert\fP\f(CR(PCRE2_SPTR\fP \f(CIpattern\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_SIZE\fP \f(CIplength\fP\f(CR,
                          uint32_t\fP \f(CIoptions\fP\f(CR, PCRE2_UCHAR **\fP\f(CIbuffptr\fP\f(CR,
                          PCRE2_SIZE *\fP\f(CIbufflenptr\fP\f(CR,
                          pcre2_convert_context *\fP\f(CIccontext\fP\f(CR);

void\fP \f(CBpcre2_converted_pattern_free\fP\f(CR(PCRE2_UCHAR *\fP\f(CIconverted\fP\f(CR);\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.sp
The first two arguments of \f(CBpcre2_pattern_convert()\fP define the foreign
pattern that is to be converted. The length may be given as
\f(CBPCRE2_ZERO_TERMINATED\fP. The \f(CIoptions\fP argument defines how the pattern is to
be processed. If the input is UTF, the \f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_UTF\fP option should be set.
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_NO_UTF_CHECK\fP may also be set if you are sure the input is valid.
One or more of the glob options, or one of the following POSIX options must be
set to define the type of conversion that is required:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD
.TP 7
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB\fP
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_WILD_SEPARATOR\fP
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_STARSTAR\fP
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_BASIC\fP
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_EXTENDED\fP
.ad l
.PD
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Details of the conversions are given below. The \f(CIbuffer\fP and \f(CIblength\fP
arguments define how the output is handled:
.sp
If \f(CIbuffer\fP is \f(CBNULL\fP, the function just returns the length of the converted
pattern via \f(CIblength\fP. This is one less than the length of buffer needed,
because a terminating zero is always added to the output.
.sp
If \f(CIbuffer\fP points to a \f(CBNULL\fP pointer, an output buffer is obtained using
the allocator in the context or \f(CBmalloc\fP\f(CR()\fP if no context is supplied. A
pointer to this buffer is placed in the variable to which \f(CIbuffer\fP points.
When no longer needed the output buffer must be freed by calling
\f(CBpcre2_converted_pattern_free()\fP. If this function is called with a \f(CBNULL\fP
argument, it returns immediately without doing anything.
.sp
If \f(CIbuffer\fP points to a non\-\f(CBNULL\fP pointer, \f(CIblength\fP must be set to the
actual length of the buffer provided (in code units).
.sp
In all cases, after successful conversion, the variable pointed to by
\f(CIblength\fP is updated to the length actually used (in code units), excluding
the terminating zero that is always added.
.sp
If an error occurs, the length (via \f(CIblength\fP) is set to the offset
within the input pattern where the error was detected. Only gross syntax errors
are caught; there are plenty of errors that will get passed on for
\f(CBpcre2_compile()\fP to discover.
.sp
The return from \f(CBpcre2_pattern_convert()\fP is zero on success or a non\-zero
PCRE2 error code. Note that PCRE2 error codes may be positive or negative:
\f(CBpcre2_compile()\fP uses mostly positive codes and \f(CBpcre2_match()\fP
negative ones; \f(CBpcre2_convert()\fP uses existing codes of both kinds. A
textual error message can be obtained by calling
\f(CBpcre2_get_error_message()\fP.
.SH "CONVERTING GLOBS"
.sp
Globs are used to match file names, and consequently have the concept of a
"path separator", which defaults to backslash under Windows and forward slash
otherwise. If \f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB\fP is set, the wildcards \f(CI*\fP and \f(CI?\fP are not
permitted to match separator characters, but the double\-star (\f(CI**\fP) feature
(which does match separators) is supported.
.sp
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_WILD_SEPARATOR\fP matches globs with wildcards allowed to
match separator characters. \f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_GLOB_NO_STARSTAR\fP matches globs with
the double\-star feature disabled. These options may be given together.
.SH "CONVERTING POSIX PATTERNS"
.sp
POSIX defines two kinds of regular expression pattern: basic and extended.
These can be processed by setting \f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_BASIC\fP or
\f(CBPCRE2_CONVERT_POSIX_EXTENDED\fP, respectively.
.sp
In POSIX patterns, backslash is not special in a character class. Unmatched
closing parentheses are treated as literals.
.sp
In basic patterns, \f(CI?\fP, \f(CI+\fP, \f(CI|\fP, \f(CI{}\fP, and \f(CI()\fP must be escaped to be recognized
as metacharacters outside a character class. If the first character in the
pattern is \f(CI*\fP it is treated as a literal. \f(CI^\fP is a metacharacter only at the start
of a branch.
.sp
In extended patterns, a backslash not in a character class always
makes the next character literal, whatever it is. There are no backreferences.
.if n .sp
.RS 4
.it 1 an-trap
.nr an-no-space-flag 1
.nr an-break-flag 1
.br
.ps +1
.B Note
.ps -1
.br
.sp
POSIX mandates that the longest possible match at the first matching
position must be found. This is not what \f(CBpcre2_match()\fP does; it yields
the first match that is found. An application can use \f(CBpcre2_dfa_match()\fP
to find the longest match, but that does not support backreferences (but then
neither do POSIX extended patterns).
